---
/**
 * TeamCard — a portrait with a frosted caption panel carrying the member's name and role.
 * The canonical implementation of Figma's `Container` / "His excellency" +
 * "Overlay+Border+OverlayBlur" component (node 8090:7151), which Home's Core Team section
 * instantiates 4x and /team 10x. Both pages previously carried their own drifted copy of this
 * markup; this component is the one place to tune it.
 *
 * Geometry confirmed against the committed node.json (design/figma/…/5891-4663 and
 * …/8187-8979 — the two files agree exactly), not eyeballed:
 *   - Card 310x380, `clipsContent: true` on the photo frame. **No corner radius and no
 *     stroke** — `cornerRadius` is absent and `strokes: []` on the card, the photo frame, and
 *     the overlay alike. The "Border" in the layer name is vestigial from the source component
 *     library; there is no border to draw.
 *   - Overlay 310x107, bottom-anchored, padding 24 all round, `layoutMode: VERTICAL`,
 *     `itemSpacing: 8`.
 *   - Overlay fills, in Figma's bottom-to-top paint order: solid black at `opacity: 0.01`,
 *     then a linear gradient rgba(0,0,0,0) → rgba(0,0,0,1) running top→bottom at layer
 *     `opacity: 0.32` — i.e. an effective transparent → `rgb(0 0 0 / 0.32)` scrim. The 1% wash
 *     is a no-op to the eye but is reproduced so the stack matches the source exactly.
 *
 * Typography: node.json specifies **Inter** (name 600/24/28.8/-0.48; role 400/16/22.4/0).
 * The rest of the site is deliberately Arial-only (`--font-family-base`, chosen in Phase 0 to
 * avoid any web-font load/FOUT), so — following the call already made and shipped for this
 * exact card on /team — the font *sizes* are honoured exactly, line-height and letter-spacing
 * are composed from the confirmed px values as one-off primitives, and the family stays
 * inherited. Introducing a second family for one repeating card isn't worth the load cost.
 *
 * A11y: `alt` is caller-supplied and defaults to "", and that default now survives real
 * headshots rather than expiring with them. Two reasons stack. The original one: the shipped
 * design uses one placeholder portrait for every member, so an identical photo repeated 14x
 * across two pages carries no information. The durable one: the card's own caption states the
 * member's name and role as real text immediately below the photo, so a filled-in `alt` would
 * make a screen reader announce the same person twice — the W3C's own "image with adjacent text
 * alternative" case, where the empty string is the correct markup, not a placeholder for it.
 * Name and role are real text, so the card is fully readable either way. The caption's contrast
 * is not gate-checked — axe cannot measure text over a raster image behind a backdrop-filter, so
 * it reports `incomplete`, not a violation — and is tracked under "Checked and passing" in
 * docs/a11y-known-issues.md for re-inspection when real headshots replace the placeholder.
 *
 * Photos are local OR remote. A member's photo arrives from the CMS as a Cloudinary URL
 * (`StaffMember.imageUrl`), which `astro:assets` cannot process without `image.remotePatterns`
 * in astro.config.mjs — and that host is not confirmed yet. So a string `src` renders as a plain
 * `<img>` with explicit dimensions and `loading="lazy"`: correct and CLS-free, just unoptimised.
 * An `ImageMetadata` `src` (the design's placeholder portrait, and any caller with a local
 * asset) still goes through the pipeline. Same call, and the same one-line reversal once the CDN
 * host is known, as `ArticleCard.astro`'s cover images.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro';

interface Props {
  /** A local asset (optimised) or a remote URL from the CMS (rendered as-is) — see above. */
  src: ImageMetadata | string;
  name: string;
  role: string;
  /** Defaults to "" (decorative) — see the a11y note above. */
  alt?: string;
}

const { src, name, role, alt = '' } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="team-card">
  {
    typeof src === 'string' ? (
      <img
        src={src}
        alt={alt}
        width={310}
        height={380}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        class="team-card__photo"
      />
    ) : (
      <Image src={src} alt={alt} width={310} height={380} class="team-card__photo" />
    )
  }
  <div class="team-card__info">
    <div class="team-card__frost" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="team-card__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <p class="team-card__name">{name}</p>
    <p class="team-card__role">{role}</p>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .team-card {
    position: relative;
    /* Square corners — the design carries no cornerRadius (see header note). overflow:hidden
       still earns its keep: it keeps the frost layers off the photo's edges. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .team-card__photo {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 310 / 380;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .team-card__info {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs); /* itemSpacing: 8 */
    padding: var(--space-md); /* 24 all round */
    color: var(--color-white);
  }

  /* ── Progressive frost ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The overlay's effect is `BACKGROUND_BLUR` with `blurType: "PROGRESSIVE"` —
     `startRadius: 0` at `startOffset.y: 0` ramping to `radius: 32` at `endOffset.y: 1`, i.e.
     no blur at the caption's top edge growing to 32px at the bottom of the card. CSS has no
     progressive backdrop-filter, so this reuses the technique already established for Home's
     hero glass (`.who__frost` in index.astro): three uniform backdrop-filters, each faded in
     by a gradient mask that starts lower than the one below it. The overlap approximates the
     ramp far more smoothly than a single masked layer, which reads as a hard band.

     Split across two hosts purely for the pseudo-element budget — three blur layers plus the
     scrim need four, and one element only offers ::before/::after. Paint order runs
     lightest → heaviest → scrim, so the fills sit on top of the frost exactly as Figma
     composites them (background blur first, node fills over it). */
  .team-card__frost,
  .team-card__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .team-card__frost::before,
  .team-card__frost::after,
  .team-card__scrim::before,
  .team-card__scrim::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .team-card__frost::before {
    backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0%, #000 45%);
  }
  .team-card__frost::after {
    backdrop-filter: blur(16px);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 35%, #000 75%);
  }
  .team-card__scrim::before {
    backdrop-filter: blur(32px);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 55%, #000 100%);
  }
  /* The overlay's two fills. Listed top-first per CSS background order, so this is Figma's
     fills[1] (the 0.32 gradient) over fills[0] (the 1% black wash). */
  .team-card__scrim::after {
    background:
      linear-gradient(to bottom, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.32) 100%), rgb(0 0 0 / 0.01);
  }
  /* Safari < 18 only ships the prefixed property, and it must live in its own @supports block:
     Lightning CSS collapses a prefixed/standard pair written in the SAME declaration block down
     to just the prefixed one, silently dropping the standard property Chrome and Firefox need.
     Chrome fails this query, so it never sees these declarations. (Same reasoning as
     index.astro's hero frost and SiteHeader's pill.) */
  @supports (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) {
    .team-card__frost::before {
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
    }
    .team-card__frost::after {
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(16px);
    }
    .team-card__scrim::before {
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(32px);
    }
  }

  /* The frost/scrim layers are positioned, so they would otherwise paint over the in-flow
     text. Promoting the text into the positioned phase puts it back on top. */
  .team-card__name,
  .team-card__role {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .team-card__name {
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 28.8px;
    letter-spacing: -0.48px;
  }
  .team-card__role {
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 22.4px;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  /* ── Mobile (node 12490:11448) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The card stops being 310 wide and fills the 350px column, but stays 380 tall — so the
     portrait crops wider rather than growing taller with the width, and four cards down a phone
     screen stay the length the design draws them. The caption keeps its 24px padding and 8px
     stack; only the two type sizes move, and both land on sizes the mobile frame states
     outright (18/24 bold name, 14/20 role) rather than on a ratio of the desktop values.
     Tracking goes to 0 with them — the desktop name's −0.48px is an Inter value the mobile
     frame does not carry. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .team-card__photo {
      aspect-ratio: 350 / 380;
    }
    .team-card__name {
      font-size: 18px;
      line-height: 24px;
      letter-spacing: 0;
    }
    .team-card__role {
      font-size: 14px;
      line-height: 20px;
    }
  }
</style>
